"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[3486],{3486:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"slug":"rozhdaemost-rossiya-2025-demograficheskoe-eho","locale":"en","title":"About 1.19 million children were born in Russia in 2025, fewer than in 1999","description":"About 1.19 million children were born in Russia in 2025, fewer than in 1999. Why births are falling and what this means for wages, pensions and maternity capital.","blocks":[{"id":"b001","kind":"paragraph","text":"About 1.19 million children were born in Russia in 2025. Rosstat has published almost no birth data since 2024, so estimates are calculated indirectly from the number of women and age-specific fertility. Demographer Alexei Raksha calculated a similar 1.18 million. That is below 1999, when 1.21 million children were born. Births have fallen for 10 consecutive years: there were 1.94 million in 2015, 1.44 million in 2020 and 1.26 million in 2023."},{"id":"b002","kind":"paragraph","text":"Birth numbers depend on 2 things: how many children each woman has on average and how many women are at the ages when births typically occur. Imagine a school admitting 1 first-grade class rather than 2 in the late 1990s. After 25–30 years, those children become parents; even if each family has as many children as the previous generation, there will again be fewer first-graders. This is a demographic echo. There were 34.2 million women aged 15–49 in 2024; Rosstat forecasts 27.4 million by 2046, almost 20% fewer."},{"id":"b003","kind":"paragraph","text":"The second reason is the number of children per woman. It is currently about 1.36, roughly comparable with the European Union\'s 1.34 and Germany\'s 1.35. Generation replacement requires about 2.1. At 1.36, each successive generation is roughly a third smaller (1.36 / 2.1 ≈ 0.65). Russia spent 1.3% of GDP on family policy in 2024, compared with slightly more than 2% on average across OECD countries."},{"id":"b004","kind":"paragraph","text":"Support is losing value for families in housing terms. In 2016, maternity capital for a second child was 453 thousand rubles, enough for about 8.4 square meters of housing. In 2026, a family first receiving capital for a second child gets about 963 thousand, 2.1 times as much. But housing has become about 3.4 times more expensive, so it buys about 5.2 square meters. First-child capital of about 729 thousand rubles buys roughly 4 square meters. From October 1, 2026, a family mortgage for a household with 1 child costs 10–12% instead of the previous 6%."},{"id":"b005","kind":"paragraph","text":"Few children today mean few workers in 20 years. Economist Rostislav Kapelyushnikov estimates employment could be lower by 7 million by 2045, about one worker in ten, or by 10.5 million in an adverse scenario. Workers gain bargaining power: labor is scarce and employers must pay more. But fewer workers will support each pensioner, and the tax base will shrink."},{"id":"b006","kind":"paragraph","text":"A family holding an unused maternity-capital certificate faces a straightforward question: use it now or wait? Since 2016, housing has appreciated faster than indexation, so each year of waiting cost square meters. Capital is indexed to inflation; a deferred certificate will buy as much as today only if housing prices rise no faster than inflation. Nobody promises how prices will behave."},{"id":"b007","kind":"heading","text":"How many children were born in Russia in 2025?"},{"id":"b008","kind":"paragraph","text":"About 1.19 million by indirect estimation; demographer Alexei Raksha calculated 1.18 million. That is below 1999\'s 1.21 million. Rosstat has published almost no birth data since 2024, so estimates use the number of women and age-specific fertility."},{"id":"b009","kind":"heading","text":"Why are fewer children being born in Russia?"},{"id":"b010","kind":"paragraph","text":"2 causes coincide. Each woman has about 1.36 children on average, while replacement requires about 2.1. There are also fewer women at typical childbearing ages because they were born during the small generations of the 1990s."},{"id":"b011","kind":"heading","text":"How much housing can maternity capital buy in 2026?"},{"id":"b012","kind":"paragraph","text":"About 963 thousand rubles for a first claim after a second child buys roughly 5.2 square meters. First-child capital of about 729 thousand buys about 4 square meters. In 2016, capital of 453 thousand rubles bought about 8.4 square meters. The payment has since increased 2.1 times, while housing became about 3.4 times more expensive."}],"sources":[{"label":"The demographic crisis returns: Russia\'s population is shrinking","url":"https://thebell.io/demograficheskiy-krizis-vernulsya-naselenie-rossii-sokrashchaetsya--i-delo-ne-tolko-v-voyne-no-i-v-sistemnom-nedofinansirovanii-semeynoy-politiki"},{"label":"Russians reminded of maternity-capital amounts after 2026 indexation","url":"https://www.rbc.ru/life/news/69ba3abc9a79474f38cf830b"}],"sourceBodySha256":"ff9748ccf2ba9fa76599dc66cba937d70782141706610d69e9984d278c697e19"}')}}]);